1
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:09,264
Last time a register saved our running sum.

2
00:00:09,264 --> 00:00:16,358
It took in a new value at each rising edge of the clock and held it still for the rest of the period.

3
00:00:16,458 --> 00:00:19,230
Today we open the box.

4
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:27,662
Our register stored a whole number, so it has several wires going in and several coming out.

5
00:00:27,762 --> 00:00:30,484
Inside there is no clever machinery.

6
00:00:30,484 --> 00:00:37,628
There is one small circuit for each bit, side by side, and all of them listen to the same clock.

7
00:00:38,083 --> 00:00:41,977
Each of these small circuits is a register for a single bit.

8
00:00:41,977 --> 00:00:48,104
It is called a flip-flop, because all it ever does is flip to one or flop back to zero,

9
00:00:48,104 --> 00:00:51,203
and it takes about ten transistors to build.

10
00:00:51,303 --> 00:00:56,921
By convention the input of a register is capital D and its output is capital Q.

11
00:00:56,921 --> 00:01:01,077
For a single flip-flop we use the small letters.

12
00:01:01,516 --> 00:01:07,286
So to understand a register of any width, we only have to understand one flip-flop.

13
00:01:07,286 --> 00:01:11,286
Let's put a probe on its three wires and watch.

14
00:01:12,516 --> 00:01:17,160
Here is the clock, and here is an input that changes whenever it likes.

15
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:21,022
Before the output appears, try to predict it.

16
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:27,628
All you need to know is that the flip-flop only looks at its input at each rising edge.

17
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:33,558
At the first edge the input is high, so the output goes high.

18
00:01:33,558 --> 00:01:40,582
During this period the input drops and comes back up, and the output takes no notice at all.

19
00:01:40,682 --> 00:01:45,916
At the second edge the input is low, so the output goes low.

20
00:01:46,350 --> 00:01:49,514
At the third edge the input is low again.

21
00:01:49,514 --> 00:01:52,427
The flip-flop does take that value in,

22
00:01:52,427 --> 00:01:57,870
but it is the value the output already has, so nothing visible happens.

23
00:01:57,970 --> 00:02:03,126
At the fourth the input is high, and the output goes high again.

24
00:02:03,583 --> 00:02:05,505
So the rule is short.

25
00:02:05,505 --> 00:02:13,211
At every rising edge q becomes whatever d is at that moment, and between edges q does not move.

26
00:02:13,311 --> 00:02:17,243
This kind is called a positive edge triggered D flip-flop.

27
00:02:17,243 --> 00:02:23,109
There is also a kind that acts on the falling edge, but we will only use this one.

28
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:28,602
That rule has a soft spot, and it is the phrase at that moment.

29
00:02:28,602 --> 00:02:36,254
Let's zoom in on one rising edge, far enough to see that the clock itself takes a little while to rise.

30
00:02:36,716 --> 00:02:41,084
Suppose the input changes right here, in the middle of the rise.

31
00:02:41,084 --> 00:02:45,320
Is the value at the edge a zero or a one?

32
00:02:45,420 --> 00:02:49,751
The transistors inside are partway through taking the old value in,

33
00:02:49,751 --> 00:02:52,336
and now they are handed a different one.

34
00:02:52,336 --> 00:02:56,398
What the output ends up as is anyone's guess.

35
00:02:56,850 --> 00:02:59,498
So the flip-flop comes with a condition.

36
00:02:59,498 --> 00:03:06,764
Its input has to be steady already a short time before the edge, so that the value has time to get in.

37
00:03:06,864 --> 00:03:09,956
That time is called the setup time.

38
00:03:10,416 --> 00:03:14,599
And the input has to stay steady for a short time after the edge,

39
00:03:14,599 --> 00:03:17,430
until the flip-flop has safely let go of it.

40
00:03:17,430 --> 00:03:20,464
That time is called the hold time.

41
00:03:20,564 --> 00:03:24,658
Together they make a window around every rising edge.

42
00:03:24,658 --> 00:03:28,066
Inside the window the input must not change.

43
00:03:28,066 --> 00:03:32,454
Outside it, the input can do whatever it wants.

44
00:03:32,916 --> 00:03:36,160
There is one more delay, on the output side.

45
00:03:36,160 --> 00:03:43,024
Even when the input behaves, the new value does not show up at the output on the edge itself.

46
00:03:43,124 --> 00:03:48,738
It appears a little later, and that delay is called the clock to q delay.

47
00:03:49,183 --> 00:03:52,595
Three numbers, then, describe a flip-flop.

48
00:03:52,595 --> 00:04:00,017
Setup and hold are demands it makes on its input, and clock to q is how long it makes its output wait.

49
00:04:01,250 --> 00:04:03,082
Let's put numbers on them.

50
00:04:03,082 --> 00:04:08,037
Take a flip-flop with a setup time of two and a half picoseconds,

51
00:04:08,037 --> 00:04:13,526
a hold time of one and a half, and a clock to q delay of one and a half.

52
00:04:13,626 --> 00:04:17,658
The clock period is thirteen picoseconds.

53
00:04:18,116 --> 00:04:20,478
Draw the window at each edge.

54
00:04:20,478 --> 00:04:26,992
It opens two and a half picoseconds before and closes one and a half after.

55
00:04:27,092 --> 00:04:32,827
This input changes many times, but every change falls outside a window,

56
00:04:32,827 --> 00:04:35,654
so each edge samples a clean value.

57
00:04:36,116 --> 00:04:37,698
Now the output.

58
00:04:37,698 --> 00:04:44,518
Before the first edge we have no idea what the flip-flop holds, so we shade it as unknown.

59
00:04:44,618 --> 00:04:51,544
One and a half picoseconds after that edge, the output shows the value the input had, which was a one.

60
00:04:51,544 --> 00:04:55,218
After the next edge it shows a zero.

61
00:04:55,650 --> 00:04:59,906
Here the hold time and the clock to q delay happen to be equal.

62
00:04:59,906 --> 00:05:06,426
Nothing forces that, because one describes the input and the other describes the output.

63
00:05:06,526 --> 00:05:13,334
In practice, though, flip-flops are built so that the hold time is the smaller of the two,

64
00:05:13,334 --> 00:05:14,620
or at most equal.

65
00:05:15,850 --> 00:05:18,820
Here are two statements to test yourself on.

66
00:05:18,820 --> 00:05:25,368
The first says that the clock to q delay is the time from the rising edge to the hold time.

67
00:05:25,468 --> 00:05:27,070
That is false.

68
00:05:27,070 --> 00:05:33,120
Clock to q runs from the rising edge to the moment the output shows the new value,

69
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:35,850
and the hold time is about the input.

70
00:05:36,283 --> 00:05:41,824
The second says that a flip-flop only updates its output at a rising edge,

71
00:05:41,824 --> 00:05:44,595
even if its input changes in between.

72
00:05:44,695 --> 00:05:49,543
That one is true, and it is the whole point of the device.

73
00:05:50,783 --> 00:05:54,037
So a register is a row of flip-flops.

74
00:05:54,037 --> 00:06:01,267
Each one samples its input in a small window around the rising edge and answers a moment later.

75
00:06:01,367 --> 00:06:07,669
Now put it back in our circuit, where the adder's result has to arrive before that window opens.

76
00:06:07,669 --> 00:06:12,607
How short can we make the clock period before the sum stops being right?

